Subject: lognormal prior to a fixed effect using the f(idx, hyper...) method does not give desired result
Message-ID: <WktZMLPYuak.dzlsKfsAFQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/WktZMLPYuak/m/dzlsKfsAFQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi!

I'm very new to both Bayesian analysis and INLA, and am trying to learn both by working through the Statistical Rethinking book homeworks that use the rethinking package (which is STAN-based) using INLA. I'm stuck on one problem because I'm unable to correctly specify the fixed effect to a lognormal distribution, and I'm getting very different results.

This is the code using rethinking (https://github.com/rmcelreath/statrethinking_winter2019/blob/master/homework/week02_solutions.pdf)

library(rethinking)
data(Howell1)
d <- Howell1
d$log_weight <- log(d$weight)
xbar <- mean(d$log_weight)

m2 <- quap(
alist(
height ~ dnorm( mu , sigma ) ,
mu <- a + b*( log_weight - xbar ) ,
a ~ dnorm( 178 , 20 ) ,
b ~ dlnorm( 0 , 1 ) ,
sigma ~ dunif( 0 , 50 )
), data=d )

precis(m2)

This is my version in INLA:

library(brinla)
library(INLA)
library(tidyverse)
data(Howell1)
d <- Howell1
log_wplot <- log(1:60)
new_logw <- bind_cols(log_wplot, NA)
colnames(new_logw) <- c("log_weight", "height")

# add the weight values of interest to the dataframe
d2.i <- d %>%
  mutate(log_weight= log(weight)) %>%
  select(c("log_weight", "height")) %>%
  rbind(new_logw) %>%
  mutate(xbar= mean(log_weight),
         log_w= log_weight - xbar,
         idx= row_number())

prec.prior <- list(prec = list(prior = "logtnormal", param = c(0, 1)))

m2.i<- inla(height ~ 1+ f(idx, log_w, model = "iid", hyper = prec.prior),
            data= d2.i,
            control.fixed = list(
        mean.intercept= 178,
        prec.intercept= 1/(20^2)),# sd = 20 --> precision =1/variance --> 1/(sd^2)
        control.compute = list(config= TRUE),
        control.predictor=list(compute=TRUE)
)

summary(m2.i)
library(brinla)
bri.hyperpar.summary(m2.i)

I get very different results. Could someone explain how I can correctly assign a lognormal prior to the fixed effect, or somehow get an equivalent result as using the rethinking package?

Thanks so much!

From uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com  Sun Aug  9 21:17:05 2020
From: uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com (Finn Lindgren)
Date: Sun, 09 Aug 2020 21:17:05 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] lognormal prior to a fixed effect using the f(idx, hyper...) method does not give desired result
Message-ID: <WktZMLPYuak.ecBSE-DtAgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <WktZMLPYuak.dzlsKfsAFQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <WktZMLPYuak.dzlsKfsAFQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/WktZMLPYuak/m/ecBSE-DtAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

If I understand the "rethinking" code correctly, it's setting a log-normal distribution on "b" itself, but the INLA code you're using is treating b as iid random effects, with a log-Normal prior on the precision parameter of that random effect. (it's not clear to me if "b" in the rethinking code is a single logNormal realisation, or several independent realisations, but that doesn't change much, since it's the distribution itself that's the main issue, but it affects what "idx" needs to be set to)

It's possible to approximate a model where the random effect itself is log-Normal, by using the inlabru package; see inlabru.org <http://inlabru.org>
The syntax for the inlabru formula is a general R expression, so it would look similar to the rethinking code.
It should be something like this:

library(inlabru)
formula <- height ~ a + exp(log_b) * log_w
components <- ~ a + log_b(map = d2.i$idx, model = "iid", hyper=list(theta=list(prior="normal", param = c(0, 1))))
result <- bru(components = components, formula = formula, family = "gaussian", data = d2.i)

inlabru will then iteratively linearise the predictor and run inla on the linearised version.

Finn

