MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

rgeneric will still only provide a Gaussian distribution, not a log-Normal one.
The current inla implementation is based on linear functionals of latent Gaussians (plus link functions to non-Gaussian observations).
inlabru goes part of the way by allowing non-linear functionals, that are linearised, which, among other things, allows transformed Gaussians to be used in linear expressions, providing non-Gaussian effects (essentially copula models).
For the cases where those linearisations provide reasonable approximations, one could in principle compute full Laplace approximations, but for now the linearised versions have been enough (and crucially didn't require any changes to the inla implementation itself).

Finn

