Subject: Clarification on combing space and time components
Message-ID: <aqklA7Q5nh0.EurJQxWXBwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/aqklA7Q5nh0/m/EurJQxWXBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Most of the literature I have read combines space and time components in a single f() call using the group parameter:

inla(y ~ -1 + Intercept + f(i, model = spde, group = i.group,
                                    control.group = list(model = "ar1")),
             data = inla.stack.data(full_stack),
             control.predictor = list(A = inla.stack.A(full_stack),
                                      compute = TRUE))

However, I have also seen somewhere of doing this in two f() calls:

inla(y ~ -1 + Intercept + f(i, model = spde) + f(group, model = "ar1")),
             data = inla.stack.data(full_stack),
             control.predictor = list(A = inla.stack.A(full_stack),
                                      compute = TRUE))

Can someone explain to me how these are different? It seems like the first method creates an interaction of space and time through the Kronecker Product but I cannot tell if this means that each space component has as its own time AR1 coefficient to be estimated.

From uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com  Tue Aug 18 17:28:34 2020
From: uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com (Finn Lindgren)
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2020 17:28:34 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] Clarification on combing space and time components
Message-ID: <aqklA7Q5nh0.VNYxBYzvBQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <aqklA7Q5nh0.EurJQxWXBwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <aqklA7Q5nh0.EurJQxWXBwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/aqklA7Q5nh0/m/VNYxBYzvBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

the short answer:

Let Cs and Ct be the covariances of a purely spatial model and a purely temporal model.

A model that using two f() components gives a functional additive model,
  \eta(s,t) = f1(s) + f2(t)
and the combined space-time covariance matrix can be written
  C = Ct \otimes 1 + 1 \otimes Cs
i.e. the sum of kronecker products, where 1 are all-one matrices of required size;
the spatial function is constant over time, and the temporal function is constant across space.

A model using a single f() with group gives a joint model
  \eta(s,t) = f(s,t)
with space-time covariance matrix
  C = Ct \otimes Cs

The first version is separable on a functional level.
The second version is only separable on the covariance level.
Both use the same set of parameters (except that the second version only needs one common precision scaling parameter).

Finn

