In-Reply-To: <yCKRpaw0tPk.9tMPOlB6BgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <yCKRpaw0tPk.9tMPOlB6BgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/yCKRpaw0tPk/m/6VVVUsN6BgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Moses,

what kind of response variable likelihood models do you have?
Depending on what they are, and depending on what latent model component are joint between the three models (or how you want to join them) it may be easier to first rewrite the models as inlabru models (inlabru.org <http://inlabru.org>), since it will then be easier to turn it into a joint model. There are currently more examples on how to build joint models that take advantage of the "copy" feature in ordinary INLA, but for three responses that can become quite messy in comparison with how an equivalent model can be setup with inlabru (inlabru handles most of the complex spatial setup internally before handing it over to inla() for the actual calculations; it means you don't have to setup you own inla.stack() calls, for example.
With some more details on the model definitions I may be able to be more specific.

Finn

