From u3eab71733027a24a at groups.google.com  Fri Aug 21 18:45:09 2020
From: u3eab71733027a24a at groups.google.com (Helpdesk)
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2020 18:45:09 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] bym2 simulation
Message-ID: <4cnAs8LKyU0.suZJI86_BgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <4cnAs8LKyU0.3VZ9eKGXBgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <4cnAs8LKyU0.3VZ9eKGXBgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/4cnAs8LKyU0/m/suZJI86_BgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This is a useful trick, as we can make a model with no data and fixed
hyperpar, and then just sample from the posterior = prior in this case.

You have to add something on the diag to make it non-singular though

data(Germany)
g = system.file("demodata/germany.graph", package="INLA")

formula = Y ~ -1 + f(region,model="bym2",graph=g,
                     scale.model = TRUE, diagonal = 1E-4,
                     hyper = list(prec = list(initial = 0, fixed =
TRUE),
                                  phi =  list(initial = 0, fixed =
TRUE)))
Germany$Y[] <- NA
r = inla(formula,family="poisson",data=Germany,E=E,
         control.compute = list(config = TRUE))

x <- inla.posterior.sample(10, r, num.threads = 1)
fun <- function() return (region)
## each column is a sample
bym2.samples <- inla.posterior.sample.eval(fun, x)
dim(bym2.samples)

